<Doc
  title="Use other MCP servers through Exeora"
  description="Configure local or remote MCP servers once and expose their tools through Exeora's MCP endpoint."
  canonicalPath="/docs/mcp-proxy/"
>
  <p>
    Exeora can act as an MCP client as well as an MCP server. The CLI connects to MCP servers
    configured on the machine, discovers their tools, and publishes those tools through the same
    Exeora MCP URL your agent already uses.
  </p>

  <p>
    The upstream connection stays on your machine. A stdio MCP process is started there, and a
    remote Streamable HTTP MCP is called from there. The gateway stores only the tool catalog it
    needs for discovery and routes calls back to the connected CLI.
  </p>

  <h2>Project MCP servers</h2>

  <p>
    Put project-specific servers in <code>exeora.toml</code>. A stdio server starts with the project
    root as its working directory, or with the selected Git workspace as its working directory when
    a call names one.
  </p>

  <pre><code>{`[mcp.servers.github]
command = "npx"
args = ["-y", "@modelcontextprotocol/server-github"]
env = { GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN = "\${GITHUB_TOKEN}" }

[mcp.servers.internal]
url = "https://mcp.example.com/mcp"
headers = { Authorization = "Bearer \${INTERNAL_MCP_TOKEN}" }`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    Values may reference environment variables as <code>{"${NAME}"}</code>. Keep secrets in the
    environment rather than committing them to <code>exeora.toml</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>User-wide MCP servers</h2>

  <p>
    Add user-wide servers to Exeora's local <code>config.json</code> under <code>mcpServers</code>.
    They are available to every project served by that <code>exeora connect</code> process.
  </p>

  <pre><code>{`{
  "mcpServers": {
    "context": {
      "command": "uvx",
      "args": ["my-context-server"],
      "env": { "API_KEY": "\${CONTEXT_API_KEY}" }
    }
  }
}`}</code></pre>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Platform</th><th>Default config path</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Linux</td><td><code>~/.config/exeora/config.json</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>macOS</td><td><code>~/Library/Preferences/exeora/config.json</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Windows</td><td><code>%APPDATA%\exeora\config.json</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    <code>EXEORA_CONFIG_PATH</code> overrides that location. Edit the existing JSON rather than
    replacing it, because it also contains the machine's other Exeora settings.
  </p>

  <h2>Override or disable a global server</h2>

  <p>
    A project entry with the same name replaces the user-wide entry for that project. Set
    <code>enabled = false</code> to exclude a global server from one project.
  </p>

  <pre><code>{`[mcp.servers.context]
enabled = false`}</code></pre>

  <h2>What the agent sees</h2>

  <p>
    Upstream tools are namespaced as <code>mcp__server__tool</code>. Exeora preserves the upstream
    input schema and MCP tool result, and adds its optional <code>workspace</code> routing argument.
    Names that would collide after normalization receive a stable suffix.
  </p>

  <p>
    On the account MCP URL, one tool name may exist in several projects. In that case Exeora adds a
    required <code>project</code> selector and validates it against the projects that actually expose
    that tool.
  </p>

  <h2>Transport and protocol compatibility</h2>

  <p>
    Configure either <code>command</code> for stdio or <code>url</code> for Streamable HTTP, never
    both. HTTP <code>Authorization</code> currently accepts Bearer tokens. Exeora prefers MCP
    2026-07-28 and automatically falls back to the legacy lifecycle for older MCP servers.
  </p>

  <p>
    If one configured MCP server is unavailable, <code>exeora connect</code> keeps serving the rest
    and prints a warning. Restart <code>exeora connect</code> after changing MCP configuration so the
    catalog and upstream connections are rebuilt.
  </p>

  <p>
    This feature proxies upstream <strong>tools</strong>. Prompts and resources from another MCP
    server are not re-published by Exeora.
  </p>
</Doc>
